# 2. Secured and monitored web infrastructure

Diagram: https://github.com/luisfe05/holbertonschool-system_engineering-devops/blob/main/web_infrastructure_design/2-secured_and_monitored_web_infrastructure.png

## What happens when a user visits www.foobar.com

DNS resolves `www.foobar.com` to the load balancer. The browser connects over
**HTTPS** (port 443). The firewall of the load balancer allows only the
needed ports, HAProxy decrypts the traffic with the SSL certificate and
forwards the request to one of the two servers (Round Robin). Each server's
firewall again allows only the needed traffic, and Nginx, the application
server and MySQL build the response. The monitoring clients on the three
servers send metrics and logs to the monitoring service.

## Infrastructure

- 3 servers: 1 load balancer (HAProxy) and 2 application servers (each with
  Nginx, an application server, the code base and MySQL; Primary on server 1,
  Replica on server 2).
- 3 firewalls (one per server).
- 1 SSL certificate for `www.foobar.com`, so the site is served over HTTPS.
- 3 monitoring clients (data collectors, e.g. Sumo Logic), one per server.

## Specifics

- **Why each additional element is added:**
  - *Firewalls:* to protect each server.
  - *SSL certificate:* to encrypt the traffic and prove the server's identity.
  - *Monitoring clients:* to collect data from each server and detect
    problems.
- **What firewalls are for:** a firewall filters network traffic according to
  rules. It allows only the required ports/IPs (for example 80, 443 and the
  SSH admin access) and blocks everything else, reducing the attack surface.
- **Why the traffic is served over HTTPS:** HTTPS (HTTP over TLS) encrypts the
  data between the user and the server, so it cannot be read or modified by
  someone in the middle (passwords, personal data), and it verifies that the
  site really is `www.foobar.com`.
- **What monitoring is used for:** to measure the health and performance of
  the infrastructure (CPU, memory, disk, response time, errors, traffic), to
  send alerts when something goes wrong and to analyze trends and capacity.
- **How the monitoring tool collects data:** a monitoring client (agent)
  installed on each server reads metrics and log files and sends them to the
  monitoring service (e.g. Sumo Logic), where they are stored, searched and
  shown in dashboards.
- **How to monitor the web server QPS (queries per second):** configure the
  monitoring client to collect the Nginx access log (or the Nginx status
  metrics, `stub_status`), count the requests received per second and chart
  them. An alert can be set when QPS goes above or below a threshold.

## Issues with this infrastructure

- **Terminating SSL at the load balancer is an issue:** the traffic between
  the load balancer and the servers is plain HTTP, so it is unencrypted inside
  the network and anyone who gets inside can read it. Also, the load balancer
  becomes a more sensitive point since it holds the private key.
- **Only one MySQL server accepts writes:** the Primary is a SPOF for writes.
  If it fails, the application cannot write until a Replica is promoted, and
  all writes are limited by the capacity of that one server.
- **All servers have the same components (database, web server, application
  server):** they compete for the same resources, so one component cannot be
  scaled alone (to scale the web part you must also duplicate the database),
  and a problem in one component (e.g. a heavy query) affects all the others
  on that server. Separating the components is better.
